# Rust Ownership Patterns

## Context
- Applied when working with struct fields that need to be moved out
- Focuses on maintaining valid struct state after moving fields
- Ensures proper ownership handling in Rust code
- Particularly important for complex data structures like RISC0 receipts
- Critical for handling proofs and receipts where partial moves are common
- Handles nested MaybePruned types in RISC0 v1.2.1 receipt claims

## Requirements
- Use `std::mem::take` when moving fields out of a struct that needs to remain valid
- Mark struct bindings as mutable when using `std::mem::take`
- Ensure field types have a valid default state for `take` to use
- Consider implementing `Default` trait for custom types that will be taken
- Use pattern matching with mutable bindings when needed
- Document ownership transfer in comments for complex cases

### RISC0 Receipt and Journal Handling
- When returning a tuple containing both Receipt and Journal:
  1. Clone the journal before using it in return values
  2. Store cloned journal in a local variable to avoid multiple clones
  3. Use the cloned journal in the return tuple
  4. Keep the original receipt intact for further use
- Example pattern:
```rust
// Good: Clone journal before use in return value
let journal = info.receipt.journal.clone();
return Ok((journal, digest, info.receipt));

// Bad: Using journal directly causes partial move
return Ok((info.receipt.journal, digest, info.receipt)); // Error!
```

- For nested receipt handling:
  1. Clone required fields before any moves
  2. Create new receipts with cloned data
  3. Maintain original receipt validity
  4. Document cloning in comments
- Example:
```rust
// Good: Clone all needed fields
let journal = info.receipt.journal.clone();
let final_receipt = Receipt {
    inner: InnerReceipt::Succinct(ident_receipt),
    journal: info.receipt.journal.clone(),
    metadata: info.receipt.metadata.clone(),
};
return Ok((journal, digest, final_receipt));
```

## Examples

<example>
// Good: Complete receipt handling with MaybePruned types
fn process_receipt(mut receipt: Receipt) -> Result<(Journal, ProofDigest, Receipt)> {
    // 1. First validate and convert receipt type
    let succinct_receipt = match &receipt.inner {
        InnerReceipt::Composite(cr) => {
            emit_event!(MetricEvents::ProofConversion,
                from => "composite",
                to => "succinct"
            );
            let sr = prover.composite_to_succinct(cr)?;
            receipt.inner = InnerReceipt::Succinct(sr.clone());
            sr
        }
        InnerReceipt::Succinct(sr) => sr.clone(),
        _ => {
            emit_event!(MetricEvents::ProvingFailed,
                error => "unexpected_receipt_type"
            );
            return Err(Error::UnexpectedType);
        }
    };

    // 2. Validate journal structure
    let journal_bytes = &receipt.journal.bytes;
    if journal_bytes.len() < 32 {
        emit_event!(MetricEvents::ProvingFailed,
            error => "invalid_journal_size"
        );
        return Err(Error::InvalidJournal);
    }

    // 3. Get digest using reference (no ownership transfer)
    let digest = match &succinct_receipt.claim {
        MaybePruned::Value(rc) => {
            match &rc.output {
                MaybePruned::Value(Some(op)) => {
                    match &op.assumptions {
                        MaybePruned::Value(ass) => ass.digest(),
                        _ => {
                            emit_event!(MetricEvents::ProvingFailed,
                                error => "pruned_assumptions"
                            );
                            return Err(Error::PrunedData);
                        }
                    }
                }
                _ => {
                    emit_event!(MetricEvents::ProvingFailed,
                        error => "invalid_output"
                    );
                    return Err(Error::InvalidOutput);
                }
            }
        }
        _ => {
            emit_event!(MetricEvents::ProvingFailed,
                error => "pruned_claim"
            );
            return Err(Error::PrunedClaim);
        }
    };

    // 4. Finally take ownership of journal
    let journal = std::mem::take(&mut receipt.journal);

    Ok((journal, digest, receipt))
}
</example>

<example>
// Good: Pattern matching with MaybePruned validation
fn extract_digest(sr: &SuccinctReceipt<ReceiptClaim>) -> Result<ProofDigest> {
    match &sr.claim {
        MaybePruned::Value(rc) => {
            // Handle receipt claim
            match &rc.output {
                MaybePruned::Value(Some(op)) => {
                    // Handle output
                    match &op.assumptions {
                        MaybePruned::Value(ass) => Ok(ass.digest()),
                        MaybePruned::Pruned(_) => {
                            emit_event!(MetricEvents::ProvingFailed,
                                error => "pruned_assumptions"
                            );
                            Err(Error::PrunedData)
                        }
                    }
                }
                MaybePruned::Value(None) => {
                    emit_event!(MetricEvents::ProvingFailed,
                        error => "missing_output"
                    );
                    Err(Error::MissingOutput)
                }
                MaybePruned::Pruned(_) => {
                    emit_event!(MetricEvents::ProvingFailed,
                        error => "pruned_output"
                    );
                    Err(Error::PrunedOutput)
                }
            }
        }
        MaybePruned::Pruned(_) => {
            emit_event!(MetricEvents::ProvingFailed,
                error => "pruned_claim"
            );
            Err(Error::PrunedClaim)
        }
    }
}
</example>

